First, I need to calculate the number of ways to choose 3 boys out of 7. The formula for combinations is C(n, k) = n! / (k! * (n - k)!). So for the boys, it would be C(7, 3). Let me compute that. 7 factorial divided by 3 factorial times (7-3) factorial. That simplifies to 7! / (3! * 4!) which is (7 × 6 × 5 × 4!) / (3 × 2 × 1 × 4!) = (7 × 6 × 5) / 6 = 7 × 5 = 35. Wait, that seems right. So 35 ways for the boys.

Next, for the girls, he needs to pick 2 out of 4. Using the same combination formula: C(4, 2). That's 4! / (2! * 2!) = (4 × 3 × 2!) / (2 × 1 × 2!) = (4 × 3) / 2 = 6. So 6 ways for the girls.

